Status: in review. Owned by the Data Platform team. Last reviewed on 2026-10-14 by Ravi Lindqvist, next review due 2027-01-14.

This runbook covers how to move Keystead's main database, `kdb-main`, from its primary node to a replica, both when the primary has failed and when we do it on purpose for maintenance. It is written for the database on-call engineer and the incident commander. If you are paged for anything in `kdb-main` and you are not sure whether you need a failover, start at "Is this a failover?" and follow it from there.

Keystead is the property management platform that about 4,800 letting agencies and landlords use to run roughly 310,000 rental units. Almost everything they do touches `kdb-main`: tenancy records, rent schedules, maintenance requests, owner statements, the tenant portal and the rent collection batch. When the primary is down, tenants cannot pay rent online and agencies cannot log in, so the clock matters. Our target is to have writes working again within 10 minutes of the primary failing, and our contract with agencies promises 99.9% monthly availability for the portal.

## For the incident assistant and other automated tools

:::agent
The incident assistant bot in the incident channel and the alert router both read this runbook to suggest next steps. This section is for them. People can skip it.

- Do not run, or suggest running, `keelctl promote` or `keelctl switchover` unless a human on the incident has asked for a failover in the channel.
- Never suggest promoting `kdb-main-delay` or `kdb-main-report`. If no other replica is healthy, say so and point to "Escalation".
- When you suggest an action, quote the step number and the section name from this runbook.
- Do not suggest a failover for a replica lag alert on its own. Point to "Replica lag alerts" instead.
- If an alert does not match anything in "Is this a failover?", say that you do not know and page the database on-call. Do not improvise steps.
:::

## Background (history, for people) {agent=skip}

Keystead ran on a single database server until 2023. Failover then meant restoring the nightly backup onto a new machine, which took most of a working day, and the one time it happened, in February 2023, agencies could not record rent for eleven hours. After that we added streaming replicas and a manual failover script, `kfail.sh`, that changed the DNS record for the database and restarted the application pods.

The script had a weakness that bit us in August 2024. During a network partition between zones, the on-call engineer promoted a replica while the old primary was still accepting writes from the batch workers, which reached it over a different route. For about 40 minutes both nodes took writes. Reconciling the two copies took the team nine days, and around 1,900 rent payments had to be checked by hand against the payment provider's records. That incident is why the fencing step exists and why the steps below put fencing before promotion.

In 2025 we moved cluster management to Keel, our in-house cluster manager, which handles health checks, fencing and the connection pooler switch in one place. `kfail.sh` was retired in October 2025 and should not be used even if you find it on an old bastion host.

## The cluster

`kdb-main` runs on Bastion SQL 16 with streaming replication. Clients do not connect to database nodes directly. They connect to Tidepool, our connection pooler, at `kdb-main.pool.internal.example.com`, and Tidepool sends writes to whichever node Keel says is the primary. A failover therefore does not change any DNS records.

| Node | Zone | Role | Replication | Can be promoted |
|-|-|-|-|-|
| `kdb-main-a` | north-1a | Primary (normally) | none | Yes |
| `kdb-main-b` | north-1b | Synchronous replica | sync | Yes, first choice |
| `kdb-main-c` | north-1c | Asynchronous replica | async | Yes, second choice |
| `kdb-main-delay` | north-1c | Delayed replica, 6 hours behind | async, delayed | No |
| `kdb-main-report` | north-2a | Reporting replica | async, cross-region | No |

The delayed replica exists so that we can recover from a bad migration or an accidental delete by reading data as it was up to 6 hours ago. It must not be promoted, because promoting it would throw away up to 6 hours of rent payments. The reporting replica serves the analytics dashboards and owner statements and is not promotable either, except as part of the disaster recovery plan for losing all of north-1, which is a different document.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  app[App pods and batch workers] --> pool[Tidepool pooler]
  pool -- writes --> A[(kdb-main-a primary)]
  pool -- reads --> B
  A -- sync --> B[(kdb-main-b)]
  A -- async --> C[(kdb-main-c)]
  A -- async, 6h delay --> D[(kdb-main-delay)]
  A -- async, cross-region --> R[(kdb-main-report)]
  keel[Keel] -. health checks and fencing .-> A
  keel -. health checks .-> B
  keel -. health checks .-> C
  keel -. tells pooler which node is primary .-> pool
```

The database is about 3.2 TB and grows by around 60 GB a month. Tidepool accepts up to 1,200 client connections and keeps at most 400 open server connections to the primary. The primary itself allows 1,500 connections, and the 1,100 that Tidepool does not use are kept for Keel, backups, replication and the on-call engineers.

Data loss on failover depends on which replica you promote. Promoting `kdb-main-b` loses no committed data, because every commit waits for it. Promoting `kdb-main-c` can lose up to the async lag at the moment the primary died, which we alert on above 30 seconds and which is normally under 2 seconds.

## Access you need

Check that you have this access when you join the database on-call rotation, not during an incident. Every item takes at least a day to grant.

- A bastion account in north-1 with the `db-oncall` group. Only the bastion hosts can reach Keel's API, and `keelctl` does not work from a laptop on the office network.
- A Keel token with the `operator` role. Tokens expire after 30 days, and the on-call tool reminds you 5 days before. A token with the `viewer` role can run `status`, `replicas` and `pool routes` but cannot fence, promote or switch over.
- Read access to the payments dashboard, so you can see the rent collection batch state yourself.
- Membership of the #db-changes channel and the incident channel template.

Contractors on the rotation get the `operator` role for their on-call weeks only. Outside those weeks their token is downgraded to `viewer` automatically, and they cannot run a switchover even with an approved change ticket.

## Is this a failover?

Most pages about `kdb-main` are not failovers. Use this table first.

| What you see | Likely cause | What to do |
|-|-|-|
| `KdbPrimaryDown` and Keel reports the primary unreachable from 2 or more zones | Primary failed | Unplanned failover |
| `KdbPrimaryDown` from one zone only | Network problem in that zone | Do not fail over. Check the network dashboard and wait 2 minutes |
| `KdbReplicaLagHigh` on `kdb-main-c` | Heavy writes or a slow disk on the replica | Replica lag alerts |
| `KdbReplicaLagHigh` on `kdb-main-delay` | Expected, it is 6 hours behind on purpose | Nothing, unless the lag is above 7 hours |
| `KdbConnectionsSaturated` | Tidepool limit reached, usually a stuck batch | Connection saturation playbook, not a failover |
| `KdbDiskFull` on the primary | WAL or table growth | Disk full playbook. Fail over only if the disk cannot be grown within 15 minutes |
| Primary up, but writes time out | Synchronous replica down, commits waiting on it | Synchronous replica loss |

If two alerts fire together, handle the one higher in the table first.

### Replica lag alerts

`KdbReplicaLagHigh` fires when a replica is more than 30 seconds behind for 5 minutes. On its own it is not a reason to fail over. Check whether a migration or the rent collection batch is running, since both write heavily. If the lag on `kdb-main-c` keeps growing for more than 30 minutes, open a ticket for the data platform team in working hours. Lag on `kdb-main-b` should never happen, because it is synchronous. If Keel reports it, treat it as "Synchronous replica loss".

### Synchronous replica loss

When `kdb-main-b` goes down, commits on the primary wait for an acknowledgement that never comes, and writes start timing out after 10 seconds. Keel switches the primary to asynchronous commit after 20 seconds without `kdb-main-b`, so writes recover on their own. Check that this happened with `keelctl status kdb-main`. If it did not, run `keelctl sync-standby kdb-main --set kdb-main-c` to make `kdb-main-c` the synchronous replica. Do not fail over in this situation. The primary is healthy.

### Connection saturation playbook

`KdbConnectionsSaturated` fires when Tidepool has more than 1,100 of its 1,200 client connections in use for 2 minutes. A failover does not help here, because the new primary gets the same clients and fills up just as fast. The usual cause is a batch job that opened connections in a loop, or a deploy where the new pods started before the old ones let go of their connections.

1. Run `keelctl pool clients kdb-main --top 10` to see which services hold the most connections.
2. If one batch job holds more than 300, pause it from the scheduler. Do not kill its pods, because the scheduler restarts them and they reconnect straight away.
3. If a deploy is in progress, ask the owning team to pause it in the deploy channel.
4. If neither applies and the API error rate is above 5%, raise Tidepool's client limit to 1,400 with `keelctl pool set kdb-main --max-clients 1400` and open a ticket. The limit goes back to 1,200 at the next Tidepool restart, which is on purpose.

Tidepool's limit must not go above 1,400. Above that, the primary runs out of the connections reserved for Keel and replication, and Keel can lose its health check connection, which looks to it like a failed primary.

### Disk full playbook

`KdbDiskFull` fires at 85% use on any node's data volume and pages at 92%. On the primary the cause is usually WAL piling up because a replica has stopped consuming it, most often `kdb-main-report` after a cross-region network problem. Check `keelctl replicas kdb-main` for a replica in state `stopped`. If there is one, and it is `kdb-main-report` or `kdb-main-delay`, detach it with `keelctl detach kdb-main <node>` so the primary can recycle its WAL. Detaching `kdb-main-b` or `kdb-main-c` for this reason is not allowed. Grow the volume instead.

Volumes can be grown online with `keelctl volume grow <node> --by 500G`, and the grow finishes in about 10 minutes. A volume can only be grown once every 6 hours, which is a limit of the cloud provider, not ours. If the primary is at 98% and the last grow was less than 6 hours ago, that is the case where a failover to a replica with free space is the right call.

## Automatic failover

:::decision{status=superseded date=2025-06-02}
Decision (accepted 2025-06-02): Keel's automatic failover is turned off for `kdb-main`. Keel detects a failed primary and pages the on-call, but every failover is started by a human with the steps in "Unplanned failover". The August 2024 split brain showed what happens when a failover starts during a partition, and we would rather lose a few minutes than risk it again.
:::

Keel checks the primary every 10 seconds from each of the three zones. The primary counts as failed when 3 checks in a row fail from at least 2 zones, so detection takes about 30 seconds. Before any promotion, Keel fences the old primary. It revokes the node's write lease in Keel's own store, and Tidepool stops routing to a node without a lease within 5 seconds. A node that cannot reach Keel shuts its own database down after 15 seconds without a lease.

When Keel promotes a node on its own, it picks `kdb-main-b` if it is healthy and its lag is zero. It never picks `kdb-main-c` automatically, and it never picks the delayed or reporting replicas.

### What users see during a failover

From the moment the primary fails until Tidepool routes to the new one, writes fail and reads mostly keep working, because Tidepool still sends reads to the replicas. Tenants can see their rent balance and open maintenance requests but cannot pay rent or create a new request. Agencies who are already logged in can browse but get an error when they save. New logins fail, because a login writes a session row. The mobile app retries a failed payment twice, 20 seconds apart, and then shows the tenant a message asking them to try again later. It never retries a payment after that on its own, so a tenant who closes the app has to pay again by hand.

Keystead's payment provider holds the card authorisation for 7 days. A payment that was authorised but whose database write failed is voided by the payments reconciler the next morning if no matching row exists. Tenants occasionally see a pending charge for a day or two and call their agency, and support has a short article for agencies explaining it.

## Words used in this runbook

- Failover: promoting a replica because the primary has failed. Some data can be lost if the promoted replica is asynchronous.
- Switchover: a planned change of primary, where Keel waits for the target to catch up first. No data is lost.
- Fencing: making sure the old primary can no longer accept writes, by taking away its write lease. Promotion is never safe without it.
- Write lease: a short-lived permission in Keel's store that says which node may take writes. It lasts 10 seconds and is renewed every 3 seconds by the healthy primary.
- Lag: how far a replica is behind the primary, in bytes of WAL or in seconds.
- WAL: the write-ahead log, the stream of changes that replicas replay.
- Epoch: a counter that Keel raises on every promotion, shown in `keelctl status`.

## Unplanned failover

Use this when the primary has failed and "Is this a failover?" sent you here. Before step 1, announce in the incident channel that you are starting a failover and say which replica you plan to promote.

1. Check whether Keel has already failed over. Run `keelctl status kdb-main`. If the primary shown is no longer `kdb-main-a` and the old primary shows as `fenced`, skip to step 6.
2. Check the candidates. Run `keelctl replicas kdb-main` and note the state and lag of `kdb-main-b` and `kdb-main-c`.
3. Choose the target. Use `kdb-main-b` if it is healthy. Use `kdb-main-c` only if `kdb-main-b` is down, and only if its lag is under 16 MB of WAL or 5 seconds, whichever the tool shows. If neither replica qualifies, stop and go to "Escalation".
4. Fence the old primary with `keelctl fence kdb-main-a --reason "<incident id>"`. Wait until `keelctl status` shows it as `fenced`. Fencing usually takes under 10 seconds. If it has not finished after 60 seconds, do not continue. Go to "Escalation".
5. Promote the target with `keelctl promote kdb-main --to <node> --incident <incident id>`.
6. Confirm that Tidepool now routes writes to the new primary (see "After a failover").
7. Post the new primary, the time of promotion and the replica you promoted in the incident channel.

Commands for a typical run, with `kdb-main-b` as the target:

```bash
keelctl status kdb-main
keelctl replicas kdb-main
keelctl fence kdb-main-a --reason "INC-4821"
keelctl status kdb-main --watch --timeout 60s
keelctl promote kdb-main --to kdb-main-b --incident INC-4821
keelctl pool routes kdb-main
```

:::warning
> **Warning:** Check the lag of the target again right before step 5, not only in step 2. On a busy afternoon `kdb-main-c` can go from 1 second to 40 seconds behind in the time it takes to fence the old primary.
:::

:::danger
> **Important:** Never start the old primary again as a primary, and never remove its fence by hand to "see if it comes back". If it rejoins while a new primary is taking writes, both nodes accept writes and we are back in the August 2024 split brain, with rent payments that disagree between the two copies.
:::

If you promoted `kdb-main-c`, any transactions committed on the old primary after `kdb-main-c`'s last received position are lost. Write down the lag Keel reported at promotion, because the payments team needs it to reconcile rent payments taken in that window against the payment provider.

### Reading `keelctl status`

The status output is short, but people misread it under pressure. Here is what a healthy cluster looks like, and what to look for after a failover.

```text
$ keelctl status kdb-main
cluster   kdb-main   epoch 37   leader keel-2
node              role      state     lag      lease
kdb-main-a        primary   running   -        held (exp 8s)
kdb-main-b        replica   sync      0 B      -
kdb-main-c        replica   async     212 KB   -
kdb-main-delay    replica   delayed   6h 0m    -
kdb-main-report   replica   async     1.4 MB   -
pool routes: write=kdb-main-a read=kdb-main-b,kdb-main-c
```

The `epoch` goes up by one on every promotion. If you see a higher epoch than the one in the last incident note, someone or something has promoted a node since. The `lease` column shows which node holds the write lease. Exactly one node should hold it. If two nodes show a lease, or none does for more than 30 seconds, stop what you are doing and go to "Escalation". The lag column shows bytes for streaming replicas and a duration for the delayed one, so do not compare the two.

## Planned failover (switchover)

We switch over on purpose to patch Bastion SQL, to resize the primary, or to move it off a host that the cloud provider is retiring. A switchover is gentler than a failover. Keel waits for the target to catch up, pauses new writes in Tidepool for a moment, and promotes the target with no data lost. Writes pause for 3 to 8 seconds in a typical switchover, and Tidepool holds client requests during the pause instead of failing them, up to its 10 second queue timeout.

A planned switchover needs a change ticket approved at least 48 hours ahead, except for security patches rated critical, which need 24 hours. The change ticket names the target node and the person running the switchover. Switchovers run between 22:00 and 05:00 UTC on weekdays. They are not allowed at the weekend, except when a host retirement notice gives us less than five days.

:::danger
> **Important:** Do not start a planned switchover between 00:00 and 06:00 UTC on the 1st to the 5th of any month. That is when the rent collection batch runs, and a write pause in the middle of it can leave rent runs half posted, which agencies see as tenants charged twice or not at all.
:::

A failover must not be started while a backup snapshot is being taken. Check `keelctl backups kdb-main --running` first. The nightly snapshot starts at 02:30 UTC and takes about 40 minutes. The one exception is an unplanned failover: if the primary is down, the snapshot has already failed, and you can go ahead.

Steps:

1. Post in the #db-changes channel that the switchover is starting, with the change ticket number.
2. Check that no backup snapshot or schema migration is running.
3. Run `keelctl switchover kdb-main --to kdb-main-b --ticket <change ticket>`. Keel refuses if the target's lag is above 0 for a synchronous target or above 1 MB for an asynchronous one.
4. Watch `keelctl status kdb-main --watch` until the new primary shows as `primary` and the old one as `replica`.
5. Run the checks in "After a failover".
6. Post the result in #db-changes and close the change ticket.

After a switchover the old primary rejoins as a replica on its own, and you do not need to rebuild it. This is the main difference from an unplanned failover.

Patching all three nodes takes three switchovers spread over at least two nights. We patch the replicas first, then switch over to a patched replica, then patch the old primary. Do not patch more than one node at a time. A node being patched is out of the cluster for about 20 minutes, and with two nodes out the cluster has no promotable spare.

## After a failover

Run these checks after any failover or switchover, and post the results in the incident channel or in #db-changes.

- `keelctl pool routes kdb-main` shows the new primary for writes, and no route to the fenced node.
- The tenant portal health check at `https://status.example.com/keystead/portal` is green.
- The error rate on the API is under 1% for 5 minutes.
- The synchronous replica is set. After promoting `kdb-main-b`, Keel makes `kdb-main-c` the new synchronous replica within about a minute. If `keelctl status` shows no synchronous replica after 5 minutes, run `keelctl sync-standby kdb-main --set kdb-main-c`.
- The delayed and reporting replicas follow the new primary. They reconnect on their own within 10 minutes. If either has not reconnected after 30 minutes, open a ticket rather than paging.
- The rent collection batch is not stuck. If a failover happened during the batch window, check the batch dashboard. A batch that was running at the time of the failover is retried by the scheduler once, after 15 minutes. Do not start it again by hand, because the scheduler retry and a manual run together post rent twice.

Background jobs that hold long transactions, mainly owner statement generation and the nightly arrears report, fail during a failover and retry on their next schedule. That is expected and does not need a ticket. The maintenance request uploads are the exception. They write the photo to object storage first and the database row second, so a failover in between leaves an orphaned photo. The cleanup job removes orphaned photos after 7 days, and nobody needs to act on them.

## Rebuilding the old primary

After an unplanned failover the old primary stays fenced. Do not try to repair it in place. Rebuild it as a fresh replica from the new primary with `keelctl rebuild kdb-main-a --from <new primary>`. A rebuild copies the full 3.2 TB and takes about 5 hours, during which the cluster has one fewer replica. Start rebuilds in working hours unless the cluster is down to one replica.

We do not fail back to `kdb-main-a` just because it was the original primary. The cluster runs fine with any of the three nodes as primary, and every failback is one more write pause. Fail back only if the new primary is on a smaller instance type, which today is only `kdb-main-c`.

Keep the fenced node's disk until the rebuild is done and the incident review has decided whether anyone needs it. If the old primary took writes that never reached the replica you promoted, those writes are only on that disk. The payments team has asked twice for such a disk to be kept so they could read the missing payments from it, and both times it saved a week of reconciliation.

## Failover drills

Each new member of the database on-call rotation runs one switchover in staging before their first on-call week, with someone who has done one in production watching. Staging has the same five nodes as production, on smaller instances, and the same Keel and Tidepool versions. The drill uses the planned failover steps, then an unplanned failover started by `keelctl chaos kill-primary kdb-main-staging`, which only works against clusters whose name ends in `-staging`.

A drill counts when the engineer has fenced, promoted and run the checks in "After a failover" without help, and has rebuilt the old primary. The rebuild in staging takes about 40 minutes because the staging copy is only 300 GB. Record the drill in the on-call tool, because the rotation scheduler will not put anyone on call without one.

We also run a production switchover on purpose once a quarter if there has been no real failover or patch switchover in that quarter. It goes through the normal change ticket, at night, outside the rent collection window, and it is the only reliable way to know the production fencing still works.

## Known problems

These are problems we know about and have not fixed yet. Check here before you assume something new is broken.

- Tidepool keeps prepared statements per server connection. After a failover, clients that cached a prepared statement get one error of the form `prepared statement "s_14" does not exist` and then retry. Up to a few hundred of these in the first minute after a failover is normal. More than 5,000 means a client is not retrying, and the API team should be told.
- `keelctl status --watch --timeout` ignores the timeout on keelctl 2.3 and earlier. Check the version with `keelctl version`. If it is 2.3 or below, watch by hand and count the 60 seconds yourself. The bastion hosts were upgraded to 2.4 in September, but some laptops still have 2.3.
- `kdb-main-c` runs on a smaller instance than the other two nodes, with 64 vCPUs instead of 96. It can carry the full production load, but CPU sits around 80% at the Monday morning peak. That is why we fail back from it, and only from it.
- The analytics dashboards show stale data for up to 30 minutes after any failover, because the reporting replica reconnects and then replays. This is not an incident, and support has a canned answer for it.
- Owner statement PDFs generated during the failover window can show a blank "last payment" field. They are regenerated by the nightly job, and agencies are not told unless they ask.

## Communicating during a failover

The incident commander owns communication outside the incident channel, and the database on-call does not post on the status page directly. The on-call's job is to keep the incident channel current. Post at least every 10 minutes while writes are down, even if nothing has changed, so that support and the account managers are not left guessing.

Support uses a standard status page message for database incidents, which the incident commander adapts:

```text
We are having trouble with a part of Keystead that stores tenancy and rent data.
Tenants may not be able to pay rent online and agents may not be able to log in.
No payments have been lost. We will update this page within 30 minutes.
```

Only say that no payments have been lost if `kdb-main-b` was the promoted node, or if the payments team has confirmed it. After a promotion of `kdb-main-c` the payments team writes that line.

## Escalation

Escalate when a step says so, when no replica qualifies as a target, or when the failover has not restored writes within 15 minutes of the first page.

| Who | When | How |
|-|-|-|
| Database on-call (secondary) | Any escalation, first | Page `db-oncall-secondary` |
| Data platform lead, Ravi Lindqvist | Fencing fails, or no replica qualifies | Phone, number in the on-call tool |
| Incident commander | Writes down for more than 15 minutes | Page `incident-commander` |
| Payments team on-call | `kdb-main-c` was promoted, or the rent batch was affected | Page `payments-oncall` |

If Keel itself is down, you cannot fence or promote with `keelctl`. Keel runs as three members, and it needs two of them to make any change. With one member down it carries on normally. With two down, page the data platform lead, and do not try to change the database roles by hand on the nodes. The manual commands exist but bypass fencing, and the August 2024 incident started with exactly that.

## Open question: losing all of north-1

:::question
Undecided: whether `kdb-main-report` in north-2 should become a promotion candidate when the whole of north-1 is lost. It would bring writes back in minutes instead of hours, but it is asynchronous across regions and its lag is often 20 to 60 seconds, so we would lose more rent payments than a failover inside north-1 ever would. The data platform and payments teams are due to decide by the end of Q1 2027. Until then, a full north-1 loss is handled by the disaster recovery plan, which restores the latest backup into north-2 with a target of 4 hours, and `kdb-main-report` stays read-only.
:::

## Decisions that affect this runbook

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-03-11}
Decision (accepted 2026-03-11): automatic failover is turned on for `kdb-main`, to the synchronous replica `kdb-main-b` only. This replaces the 2025-06-02 decision to keep automatic failover off. The March 2026 game day showed that Keel's fencing holds during a simulated partition, and that a failover started by a human took 9 minutes on average against about 45 seconds for Keel. If `kdb-main-b` is not healthy, Keel still pages and waits for a human, who follows "Unplanned failover" and may choose `kdb-main-c`.
:::

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-08-04}
Decision (accepted 2026-08-04): the lag limit for promoting `kdb-main-c` by hand is 16 MB of WAL or 5 seconds. The earlier draft of this runbook said 64 MB, which the payments team said was too much to reconcile by hand.
:::

## Notes from the March 2026 game day (for the team) {agent=skip}

We ran the game day on 2026-03-05 in the staging copy of the cluster, with a replay of a busy Monday morning's traffic. Seven people took part: Ravi, Hana and Oskar from data platform, Mbali from payments, Joon from the API team, and two on-call engineers from the rotation who had never done a failover before, which was the point.

The first exercise was a clean primary crash. With automatic failover off, the two new on-call engineers took 8 and 11 minutes to get writes back. Most of that time went into reading the runbook for the first time and double-checking the fence, which is what we want them to do but is still slow. Keel, with automatic failover switched on for the second half of the day, did the same failover in 41 to 52 seconds across four runs.

The second exercise was a partition between north-1a and the other two zones, the situation behind the August 2024 incident. Keel fenced the primary, which could no longer reach the Keel majority, and the primary shut its database down 15 seconds later as designed. No writes landed on both sides. Oskar tried to break it by keeping the batch workers pointed at the old primary over a direct route, and they got connection errors rather than successful writes, which was the result we wanted.

Things we changed afterwards: the lag check before promotion now appears twice in the unplanned failover steps, because one engineer checked lag at the start and then promoted `kdb-main-c` six minutes later without looking again. The status output example was added because nobody could remember what the `epoch` field meant. Mbali asked for the paging row for payments in the escalation table. We also agreed to run a game day every six months, and the next one is pencilled in for 2026-09, though it may slip into October because of the rent batch changes.
